<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  Bind ClickHouse to IPv4 explicitly.

  The official image ships docker_related_config.xml with <listen_host>::</listen_host>,
  which binds the IPv6 wildcard and relies on it also accepting IPv4. On a host
  with IPv6 disabled that fails for EVERY listener:

    Application: Listen [::]:8123 failed: DNS error: EAI: Address family for
    hostname not supported

  ClickHouse then starts but serves nothing, the entrypoint's own init step times
  out ("ClickHouse init process timeout"), and the healthcheck reports unhealthy —
  which in turn blocks the collector, Observatory and nginx behind it. The failure
  is loud in ClickHouse's err.log and invisible everywhere else, so it reads as
  "the stack is broken" rather than "IPv6 is off on this box".

  Found on a host with IPv6 disabled. Hard-coding IPv4 is the portable choice: an
  air-gapped network is at least as likely to have IPv6 turned off as on, and
  nothing in this stack needs IPv6.
-->
<clickhouse>
    <!-- replace="replace" is required. ClickHouse MERGES config.d files, so a
         plain <listen_host> APPENDS to the image default (::) rather than
         overriding it. The server then attempts two binds and the second
         fails with "Address already in use: 0.0.0.0:8123" — after which it
         keeps running with no HTTP listener at all, so the container looks
         alive while refusing every connection. -->
    <listen_host replace="replace">0.0.0.0</listen_host>
</clickhouse>
